WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.030
From his debut appearance as the central character
in a disturbing episode of Atlanta to his

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:10.220
wildly mysterious arrival at the 2018 Emmy
Awards ceremony, there's a lot we don't know

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.580
about Teddy Perkins.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.330
Here's a look behind the scenes to help fill
in the blanks.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.330
Consistently surreal

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.980
"Teddy Perkins" might have been a standout
episode of Atlanta, but as weird as it is,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.319
it was fully in keeping with the eerie surrealism
established in the world of the show.

00:00:25.319 --> 00:00:29.580
Glover and his Atlanta team have never been
afraid to tread toward the surreal in the

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.450
world of the show, there's a fuzziness on
the boundaries of reality just outside the

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:33.940
camera frame.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.280
"Oh s---, it's Justin Bieber!"

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.600
But the "Teddy Perkins" episode stands apart
in the Atlanta universe, both in its unsettling

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.580
use of heavy whiteface makeup as a metaphor
for the corrosive effects of fame and racism,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:57.200
and as a Frankenstein's monster of cinematic
references, many pulled from classic horror.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.920
References aplenty

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.530
The episode starts off like a fairly normal
Atlanta episode Lakeith Stanfield's Darius

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.000
rents a truck to pick up a free piano from
a wealthy eccentric.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.950
As soon as Darius arrives at Teddy Perkins'
mansion, however, the tone of the episode

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:12.540
changes.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.900
Darius is stepping into a horror movie, and
even his wardrobe reflects that.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.979
Director Hiro Murai told Vanity Fair that
he wanted his outfit to evoke James Caan's

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:26.939
look in the 1990 film adaptation of Stephen
King's Misery, a movie about a trapped and

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.650
infirm talent held hostage.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:29.760
Sound familiar?

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.540
When Darius comes across Teddy's wheelchair-bound
brother Benny in the basement, he's covered

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:39.999
in bandages and wearing sunglasses, just like
the Invisible Man from the 1933 horror classic

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:40.700
of the same name.

00:01:41.209 --> 00:01:45.360
And in another of the episode's classic horror
movie references, there's shades of Psycho

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.380
when a relative who may or may not exist finally
appears, also in a basement.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.400
"Ah!"

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.880
There's a sly reference to another Lakeith
Stanfield role in the modern-day horror classic

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:58.880
Get Out.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.540
In Get Out, Stanfield plays Logan, a mind
control/body swapping victim who's briefly

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.540
woken up by a camera flash in one of the tensest
moments in a film full of them.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.680
"Get out!

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.120
Yo, chill man, chill GET OUT.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:13.620
Chill"

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.780
In the "Teddy Perkins" episode of Atlanta,
Teddy takes Darius' photo suddenly with a

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:18.800
bright flash.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.140
"I'm not a big photo person."

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.520
The most obvious cinematic reference, however,
is the 1962 psychological thriller–horror

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.700
film Whatever Happened to Baby Jane?

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.330
The film follows two sisters, one obsessed
with her childhood fame as a vaudeville actor

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:38.240
and the other left paraplegic after an accident
that she blames on her sister.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.090
"You wouldn't be able to do these awful things
to me if I weren't still in this chair!"

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.090
The Jackson connection

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.540
Visually, Teddy Perkins is most obviously
inspired by Michael Jackson.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:52.260
But Teddy doesn't just resemble the King of
Pop: he tells Darius about his domineering

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:57.240
father with pride in his voice, explicitly
comparing him to Michael's famously abusive

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.890
father, Joe Jackson.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:03.110
Michael Jackson's brother Jermaine wrote a
book largely defending the abuse they suffered

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:04.200
at their father's hands.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.960
By combining Jermaine's defensiveness about
his father with a nod to Michael's alleged

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.150
skin condition, it further confuses who Teddy
Perkins is and makes the twist that Benny

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.660
is actually still alive hit even harder.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.130
Joe Jackson isn't the only abusive father
to get a shoutout in the episode; Teddy also

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.820
references Marvin Gaye Sr., father of singer
Marvin Gaye.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.200
Gaye was shot to death by his father, and
Teddy's reference to him foreshadows the violent

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.280
ending of the episode.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.680
So who is Teddy Perkins?

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.080
It might have taken at least a couple viewings
before you realized it, but yes, that's definitely

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.770
Atlanta star and creator Donald Glover playing
the role of Teddy Perkins, a bone-chilling

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.530
allegory for the perils of black fame in America.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:49.851
The secret punchline of the entire episode,
however, is that when the credits roll, it's

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.760
revealed that Teddy Perkins was played by
"himself."

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:57.680
Teddy is presented as an entity singularly
removed from Glover, something that remained

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.770
consistent even while the episode was filming,
according to Derrick Haywood, the actor who

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:02.600
plays Benny.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:03.840
As Haywood recalled,

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:07.050
"They were calling him Teddy, he acted as
Teddy.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.060
There was no Donald on set whatsoever."

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:10.520
Teddy's night out

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:16.620
The "Teddy Perkins" character actually showed
up at the 2018 Emmys, even going so far as

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.639
to pose for pictures with Donald Glover, indicating
that another actor got the Teddy Perkins treatment

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:22.280
for the big night.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.720
When asked who it was, Atlanta cast members
continually referred to Teddy as a real guy,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.290
not merely a role that Glover played, keeping
the weirdness alive for at least one more

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:33.290
night.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.510
Much like the episode bears his name, it seems
like the mystery of the Teddy Perkins character

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:38.710
eludes easy answers.

